export const SAMPLE_TEXT = "Imagine a reporting app that can render output for two targets: a web page and a plain-text terminal. Each target needs its own versions of several components: a heading, a table and a warning box. The web heading must not be mixed with the terminal table, or the output breaks. If you use a set of characters, check every new reference image against the character sheet, character by character: outfit, hands or paws, accessories, hair, colors and height ratio. Image editing tools silently change details you did not ask for, and the video model copies the mistake into every frame. Our guide to consistent AI characters covers how to build the sheet.\nNewer models often hallucinate less on common questions, and some are trained to admit uncertainty more often. Rates still differ by topic and language, so test a model on your own kind of question before you rely on it, and keep a human check where errors are costly. Lay the full original song on the timeline first, then drop each segment at its planned start time. Put cuts on downbeats or on the strong events you marked. A cut a few frames early or late is easy to feel on a chorus drop.\nTo create subtitles, transcribe the final dialogue track and fix the text by hand. For example, cv.cm's speech-to-text runs Whisper in the browser and exports SRT or VTT. YouTube accepts both SRT and WebVTT caption files, but burned-in subtitles are safer for platforms where viewers rarely turn captions on. AI does not decide what your video is about. It cannot tell which 60 seconds of a long talk are the most interesting to your audience, and it does not know which pause is a dramatic beat and which is dead air. That judgment stays with you.\nPlatforms are adding their own tools. YouTube's automatic dubbing creates translated audio tracks and marks those videos as auto-dubbed. Its help page notes that dubs can contain errors in pronunciation, proper nouns, idioms and jargon, and it lets creators review dubs before they are published. Use that review step, or upload your own checked track. If you want the speaker's mouth to match the new language, see our guide to AI lip sync. Dijkstra's algorithm is fast but requires non-negative edge weights. Some problems naturally have negative weights: a refund, a gain in a currency exchange, or an energy cost that can go below zero. Bellman-Ford handles these graphs correctly and also tells you when no shortest path exists because of a negative cycle, a loop you could go around forever to make the cost smaller and smaller.\nGrooms use the same base rules: body angled, weight on the back leg, hands busy. Good groom poses are buttoning the jacket, adjusting cufflinks, hands in pockets with thumbs out, leaning on a wall, and walking. A camera-shy groom can be photographed from behind or in detail shots first. Gemini offers an OpenAI-compatible base URL, and many other providers, including DeepSeek and several Chinese and open-model hosts, accept the chat completions format. Compatibility is usually partial: advanced features such as structured output, tool calling or image input may differ. Read each provider's compatibility page before switching with only a URL change.\nA seed does not hold a character across different scenes. Change the pose, the background or the framing, and the same seed produces a different layout and often a different face. It also stops working when you switch model, tool version, hardware or sampler. Treat a seed as a way to repeat a picture, not as a character identity. Aperture and shutter speed change the amount of light that reaches the sensor. ISO does not add light. It amplifies the signal after capture, so it brightens the image but also brightens the noise. This is why photographers usually choose aperture and shutter first and raise ISO last.\nFocal length is an optical distance, not the physical length of the lens. It is the distance from the lens's optical centre to the sensor when the lens is focused at infinity. A longer focal length projects a larger image of the same scene, so the sensor sees a narrower slice of it: the angle of view gets smaller and distant things look bigger. Restaurant lights are often warm and dim, so food can look orange, and a hard overhead lamp can throw a dark shadow under the plate. Before you sit down, look for a table by the window. At night, choose a seat where the main light falls on the table from the side rather than straight above. Use a white napkin or the menu as a reflector to lighten the shadow side.\nFor the clicks in a specific tool, see the step-by-step tutorial on removing a photo background. For example, cv.cm's remove-background tool runs BiRefNet in the browser, so the photo is not uploaded, and lets you export a transparent PNG or place the subject on white, a color or a blurred copy of the original. The result is a model that removes compression blocks and noise while it enlarges, which is why it works well on old web images, screenshots and phone photos. Separate weights are usually trained for anime and illustration, because flat colors and clean lines need different texture than photographs.\nThe prompt is first turned into numbers by a text encoder, a language model trained to represent the meaning of captions. Inside the denoising network, a mechanism called cross-attention lets every region of the image look at the prompt's words at every step. A region that is becoming a face pays attention to words like \"freckles\" or \"smiling\". Be realistic about what AI video does well at the time of writing: short shots, simple actions, mood and atmosphere. It still struggles with readable text, hands doing precise tasks, several characters interacting, and long continuous takes. Plan your video around short, clear shots and the results will look much better than a single ambitious prompt.\nTreat the first element as a sorted list of length one. Take the second element and insert it before or after the first. Take the third element and insert it into the right place among the first two. Continue until every element has been inserted. YouTube does not require disclosure for clearly unrealistic content, minor edits such as color correction or filters, or AI help with scripts, titles and captions, and it says that disclosure alone does not limit a video's audience or monetization. TikTok says a label does not make harmful or misleading content acceptable.\nChaining has a cost. Small errors build up: a slightly softer face at the end of shot A becomes the starting face of shot B, and after several links the character can drift. Re-anchor every few shots by starting from a fresh keyframe made from the original reference, not from the last frame. Most of the time those guesses are good. Your job is to give the phone good light and a steady shot, and to correct it when it guesses wrong: when it focuses on the background, when the sky turns white, or when a dark scene looks grey. The habits below fix those cases on any modern phone.\nTreat these as starting points. Take a shot, zoom in on the screen and look at the histogram. Turn on highlight warnings: the brightest areas should not flash. If they do, use a faster shutter speed. Bracket by one stop either way and pick the best later. A pre-wedding photoshoot is a portrait session of the couple, done weeks or months before the wedding. In China, Taiwan, Hong Kong, Korea, Japan, Vietnam, Singapore, Malaysia, Indonesia and India it is a major part of wedding planning. The photos are often printed large for the wedding venue, made into an album, used on invitations, or shown as a slideshow at the reception. In many Western countries the same idea is called an engagement shoot, and it is usually shorter and more casual.\nA language model does not know your situation. It only sees the text you send. When a prompt is vague, the model fills the gaps with the most typical answer, which is usually generic, too long or aimed at the wrong reader. Prompt engineering is mostly the work of removing guesses. Imagine two lines that cut the picture into three equal parts from left to right, and two more that cut it into three equal parts from top to bottom. You now have nine boxes and four points where the lines cross. Camera makers often call these crossing points the strong points or hot spots of the frame.\nRun this in a module script or, better, in a Web Worker. Model inference can block for hundreds of milliseconds or more, and in a worker the page stays responsive. WebGPU is also available inside workers. Show download progress to the user, because the first load of a large model can take a while on a slow connection. The line is only half of the hook. The first frame matters as much. In our tests with AI-generated shorts, the opening frame decides much of the early drop-off: it should already be the best-looking frame of the video, bright, with the subject's face fully visible and lit, already moving or reacting. Slow reveals, a figure looking down, dim light or a waking-up intro lose viewers before the first line ends. Put the concept into the setting and props instead of a slow opening, and keep a close or medium shot for the first seconds so the subject fills most of the frame.\nA camera meter tries to make what it sees a middle grey. When a white dress fills a large part of the frame, the meter thinks the scene is too bright and darkens it. The dress turns dull grey and the face looks muddy. The fix is to tell the camera to make the picture brighter, while watching that the brightest part of the dress keeps its detail. Guidance works by comparing two predictions at each step, one with your prompt and one without, and pushing the result further toward the difference. That is why the scale feels like a strength dial for the prompt, and why setting it too high exaggerates everything.\nWindows come in two kinds. A fixed-size window of k elements adds the element entering on the right and removes the one leaving on the left, for example to compute a moving average. A variable-size window grows and shrinks to satisfy a condition, as above. Many tools take duration, resolution and aspect ratio as settings rather than text. OpenAI's guide notes that these technical parameters belong in the API call or interface, not in prose. Put them in text only when the tool's documentation says the model reads them there.\nGPS tags are the main risk. A phone camera with location access records latitude and longitude to within a few meters, so a photo taken at home and shared as an original file can reveal the address. The timestamp and device model add context, and a serial number can tie several anonymous photos to the same camera.";
